# Suresh induction follows the Station D debrief: etomidate or low-dose fentanyl + midazolam; ketamine avoided
SU2_SHORT = [['Etomidate, rocuronium, vent pre-set', 'Intubated, SpO₂ 92%'], ['Propofol and suxamethonium', 'Arrest on induction'],
             ['Ketamine', 'Tachycardia, hypertension: HR 168'], ['Full-dose midazolam and fentanyl', 'BP 45/25']]

# Station D debrief (from the station sheet)
SURESH_DEBRIEF = [
  ('Shock recognition', 'What distinguishes cardiogenic from distributive (septic) shock in this child?', 'Cardiogenic: raised JVP, hepatomegaly, cool peripheries, fluid-unresponsive, elevated BNP/troponin, low EF on echo. Septic: warm peripheries, low SVR, flat JVP, fluid-responsive (early).'),
  ('Inotrope choice', 'Why is fluid potentially harmful in cardiogenic shock, and what is your first inotrope?', 'Fluid overloads a failing ventricle and worsens pulmonary oedema and MR. First choice: low-dose adrenaline (0.05–0.1 mcg/kg/min) or milrinone (reduces afterload, inotrope); avoid pure vasoconstrictors.'),
  ('Safe intubation', 'Why did you avoid ketamine for induction in cardiogenic shock?', 'Ketamine is a sympathomimetic: tachycardia and hypertension; in a failing myocardium with HR 168 this is dangerous. Use etomidate (haemodynamically neutral) or low-dose fentanyl + midazolam.'),
  ('Post-intubation management', 'Why did BP fall after intubation and how do you manage it?', 'IPPV reduces venous return (preload): cardiac output falls in a volume-depleted or failing heart. Cautious 5 mL/kg fluid, reduce PEEP, vasopressor up-titration. Expected: anticipate before intubation, not react after.'),
  ('Escalation', 'At what point would you consider mechanical circulatory support (ECMO/VAD)?', 'Refractory cardiogenic shock: MAP < 50 despite 2 vasopressors, lactate rising despite treatment, OI > 16, deteriorating echo. Early referral to a centre with ECMO/VAD; Berlin Heart for chronic cardiomyopathy.'),
  ('Communication', 'How do you communicate urgency to the cardiology team when calling for help?', 'SBAR + "I need you here now": state BP, lactate, EF; never "when you get a chance"; document the time of the call; escalate to the attending if the specialist is delayed > 15 minutes.'),
]

